Upon heating, D0 shows a \(4.5\%\) linear unit cell volume increase up to \(493\;\mathrm{K}\) . This is congruent to the behavior of the isoreticular, literature-reported \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{BME-bdc})_{2}\mathrm{dabco}\) \(\mathrm{BME=}\) 2,5-bismethoxyethoxy) which volume increases by \(4.5\%\) upon heating to \(463\;\mathrm{K}^{17}\) . Pt nanoparticles gradually decreases this to \(3.9\%\) (D1 and D2) and \(3.0\%\) (D3). It is important to note, however, that for all materials two distinct temperature regions of thermal responsiveness can be observed; a low temperature region (up to approximately \(403\;\mathrm{K}\) ) where all materials display linear expansion behavior and a notable slope change in parameter evolution above this temperature for one or more unit cell parameters or principal directions, see Fig. 4. In the following, we will first elaborate on the linear TE region, then discuss the high-temperature behavior.
